# A Jenga block: it falls under gravity, rests on whatever holds it, goes over when it isn't held properly, and you can drag it with the stylus.
#
# Set-up in the editor (the way the Jenga project is built):
#   1. A MeshInstance3D named JengaBlock, Scale 1, Rotation 0, with this script attached. Its origin can be anywhere (the block's underside is fine).
#   2. A CollisionShape3D box named JengaCollision under it, Solid turned on, LONGER IN Z THAN IN X (0.6 x 0.3 x 1.8 here) and lifted so it sits on the block.
#      To lay a layer across the one below, turn the JengaCollision 90 (or -90) about Y, not the block: the script reads that turn.
#      Set shape_lift, half_length and half_height below to the shape's numbers (its Y position, half its length in Z, half its height).
#   3. A TouchArea3D named JengaTouch under the block, a box a little bigger than the block so a fingertip finds it easily.
#   4. Solid CollisionShape3D nodes (Solid turned on) for the table.
#   5. One Node3D named Tower, anywhere in the scene. It is not a thing in the game: the blocks use it to talk to each other (they keep a count of how many blocks
#      are moving in its position.y, and a "the tower is coming down" flag in its position.x). Leave it at 0, 0, 0.
#   To have many blocks: build one, attach the script, then duplicate it (Ctrl+D). Each copy keeps its own children, and `$JengaTouch` and `$JengaCollision`
#   mean the ones under THAT block.
#   The 3D scene must be on the BOTTOM screen (the touch screen): in the project's toolbar set "2D screen" to Top.
#
#   Stylus  touch a block and drag it across the table. Only ONE block is picked up at a time. Let go and it drops.
#   B       put the block back where it started.
#
# How it behaves. Every block starts by settling: it falls onto whatever is under it. Then it looks at what holds it (every check_interval seconds, each block at
# its own moment): three points just below its underside (near each end, and the middle), taken along the shape's longer side.
#   - Both ends are held, or the middle and an end: it stays put (asleep: it does nothing but count down to its next look).
#   - Only one end is held: the centre is over nothing, so it goes over: it TIPS about the held end and the other end drops.
#   - Only the middle is held: it is BALANCED on one block, and that is not stable: it goes over the edge of the block under it, toward the side that just
#     lost its support (a hash of its height when that isn't known, so a whole layer goes the same way).
#   - Nothing under it: it falls.
#   Going over is a tip when nothing is on top of the block. A block with something resting on it can't tip (the side that rose would push up into what is on it),
#   so it SLIDES out from under its load along its length instead.
#   A tipping block that its far end doesn't catch (past tip_limit), a sliding block that comes off its support and one that loses its support LET GO: they carry the
#   motion they had (drifting the way they were going, and turning end over end) and fall, skid and tumble across whatever is below. A turn is refused when it would push a
#   corner into something, so a tumbling block bounces off what it hits instead of sinking into it. A block that has slowed down is looked at again like any other:
#   still not held properly, it goes over some more; held, it sleeps.
#   The load above a block that has gone loses its support and lets go in its turn: that is how a tower comes down.
#   When collapse_count blocks are moving at once the tower is COMING DOWN: every block that is still at rest is thrown outward, popping up and spinning, all at
#   once. Pulling one block out, or a block or two toppling, doesn't do this; several going together does.
#
# What it costs on the DS (measured): a look is one probe_solid call, about 0.5 to 0.8 ms with 50 solid blocks in the scene. A block that is moving also pays for
# move_and_collide, about 0.6 to 1.6 ms, and a turn in the air a second one: a tower that is coming down is slower than one standing still.

var gravity = -30.0         # units/s^2, downward
var max_fall = 30.0         # the fastest it falls, so it can't skip through a floor
var shape_lift = 0.2609     # how far up the collision shape's centre is from the block's origin
var half_length = 0.9       # half of the shape's length (its Z)
var half_height = 0.15      # half of the shape's height
var balance_reach = 0.3     # half the width of the block under a block that is balanced on it: the edge it tips over
var end_inset = 0.15        # the end probes sit this far in from each end: a block must rest on more than its very tip to count as held
var look_below = 0.06       # how far under the underside to look for support (the physics body rests 0.01 above what holds it)
var tip_start = 40.0        # degrees/s a tip starts at
var tip_accel = 900.0       # degrees/s^2 it speeds up at (a long block pivoting on its end)
var max_spin = 80.0         # the fastest a tip turns, degrees/s: about 3 degrees a frame, so the far end moves under 0.06 a frame and the next look sees it touch what it lands on (a tip is placed, not collision-tested)
var tip_limit = 45.0        # tipped this far and still not caught: it lets go of the edge
var slide_start = 1.0       # units/s a slide starts at
var slide_accel = 5.0       # units/s^2 it speeds up at
var max_slide = 2.5         # the fastest it slides: a block pulled out from under a tower is thrown out, but not off the table
var calm = 1.0              # seconds after the scene starts during which blocks only settle (a block that decides what to do before the blocks above it have landed decides wrongly)
var slide_look = 0.1        # seconds between the looks of a block that is sliding (it looks every frame while tipping)
var launch_push = 1.2       # units/s a block that lets go of a tip drifts at, the way it is falling
var launch_spin = 220.0     # degrees/s a block that lets go starts turning at
var collapse_count = 4.0    # this many blocks moving at once (after the first calm second) and the whole tower comes down
var blow_speed = 2.0        # how hard a resting block is thrown outward when the tower comes down (units/s, more the farther it is from the middle of the table)
var blow_pop = 1.0          # units/s upward that goes with it
var impact_speed = 2.5      # a block landing faster than this (units/s: a fall of about 0.1) is shaken by the impact
var impact_kick = 1.4       # units/s of sideways speed, up to, that the impact gives it (in a direction that looks random), and some spin: what knocks a stack off its true
var bounce = 0.3            # how much of its speed a block keeps when it hits a wall, or a turn is refused
var ground_friction = 5.0   # 1/s: how fast a block skidding on something slows down
var rest_speed = 0.35       # a block skidding slower than this (units/s) and turning slower than 25 degrees/s has stopped
var settle_lift = 0.05      # a block that stops tipping is lifted this much and dropped again, so a corner that went a little into what it landed on comes out
var check_interval = 0.2    # seconds between the looks of a block that is at rest: the shorter, the faster a collapse spreads, and the more it costs (a look is about 0.5 ms)
var max_drag_step = 0.4     # the most the block follows the stylus in one frame: a fast flick can't teleport it through the tower

var mode = 1                # 0 = at rest, 1 = falling / skidding / tumbling, 2 = tipping about a held end or an edge, 3 = sliding out from under a load
var vx = 0.0                # the sideways speed it carries while it falls and skids (units/s, world X and Z)
var vy = 0.0                # falling speed (units/s)
var vz = 0.0
var spin = 0.0              # how fast it is turning about its cross axis (degrees/s, with a sign), or, sliding, how fast it slides (units/s)
var slide_side = 0.0        # sliding toward the shape's +Z end (+1) or its -Z end (-1)
var pivot = 0.0             # where along the shape's length (from its centre) it is tipping about: an end that is held, or the edge of the one block it is balanced on
var tilt_sign = 0.0         # which way it is tipping: +1 raises the shape's +Z end, -1 lowers it
var last_held = 0           # what held it at its previous look (1 = the -Z end, 2 = middle, 4 = the +Z end, added up)
var joined = false          # this block has been thrown by the collapse that is going on (it is thrown once per collapse)
var gone = false            # this block has fallen off the table for good: it is out of the game and does nothing more
var gone_depth = 20.0       # how far below where it started a block has to be to be gone
var timer = 0.0             # counts down to the next look while at rest
var age = 0.0               # seconds since the scene started
var roof_timer = 0.0        # counts down to the next look at what is on top of a block that is tipping
var start_x = 0.0
var start_y = 0.0
var start_z = 0.0

# Which way the block lies, read from the turn of its collision shape: the horizontal direction of the shape's +Z end (dir_x, dir_z), and how a tilt of the
# block (its +Z end going up) is written into rotation.x and rotation.z.
var dir_x = 0.0
var dir_z = 1.0
var tilt_x_scale = -1.0
var tilt_z_scale = 0.0
var tilt = 0.0              # the block's tilt in degrees, and its sin and cos, kept so a frame doesn't work them out again
var tilt_sin = 0.0
var tilt_cos = 1.0

var dragging = false        # the stylus is holding the block
var drag_y = 0.0            # height of the flat plane the block is dragged along (its own height when it was picked up)
var grab_dx = 0.0           # where on the block the stylus took hold, so the block doesn't jump to centre under the stylus
var grab_dz = 0.0

# Changes what the block is doing, keeping the count of moving blocks on the Tower node (position.y) and clearing the collapse flag (position.x) when nothing moves.
func set_mode(m: int):
    if mode == 0 and m != 0:
        $Tower.position.y = $Tower.position.y + 1.0
    elif mode != 0 and m == 0:
        var left = $Tower.position.y - 1.0
        if left < 0.0:
            left = 0.0
        $Tower.position.y = left
        if left < 2.0:
            $Tower.position.x = 0.0
    mode = m

# The tower is coming down and this block was at rest: it is thrown outward (away from the middle of the table, and a little at random) and upward, spinning.
func blow():
    joined = true
    set_mode(1)
    var a = sin(age * 53.0 + position.x * 83.0 + position.y * 19.0)
    var b = cos(age * 67.0 + position.z * 71.0 + position.y * 29.0)
    vx = (position.x * 1.5 + a * 0.6) * blow_speed
    vz = (position.z * 1.5 + b * 0.6) * blow_speed
    vy = blow_pop * (0.5 + 0.5 * a * b)
    spin = a * b * launch_spin

# Tips the block to `angle` degrees about its cross axis (and remembers the sin and cos).
func set_tilt(angle: float):
    tilt = angle
    rotation.x = tilt_x_scale * angle
    rotation.z = tilt_z_scale * angle
    tilt_sin = sin(angle)
    tilt_cos = cos(angle)

func _ready():
    start_x = position.x
    start_y = position.y
    start_z = position.z
    var shape_yaw = $JengaCollision.rotation.y
    if abs(shape_yaw) > 45.0:
        # Laid along X.
        dir_x = 1.0
        if shape_yaw < 0.0:
            dir_x = -1.0
        dir_z = 0.0
        tilt_x_scale = 0.0
        tilt_z_scale = dir_x
    $Tower.position.y = $Tower.position.y + 1.0   # it starts out settling, which is moving
    # Blocks look at different moments, so a tower doesn't do all its looking in the same frame.
    timer = check_interval * (0.5 + 0.5 * sin(position.x * 57.0 + position.y * 31.0 + position.z * 17.0))

func reset_block():
    position.x = start_x
    position.y = start_y
    position.z = start_z
    set_tilt(0.0)
    vx = 0.0
    vy = 0.0
    vz = 0.0
    spin = 0.0
    dragging = false
    set_mode(1)

# Turns the block to `new_tilt` degrees about the point of it that is `reach` along the shape's length and `rise` above the block's origin: that point stays put.
func tilt_about(reach: float, rise: float, new_tilt: float):
    var along = reach * tilt_cos - rise * tilt_sin
    var up = reach * tilt_sin + rise * tilt_cos
    var pivot_x = position.x + dir_x * along
    var pivot_y = position.y + up
    var pivot_z = position.z + dir_z * along
    set_tilt(new_tilt)
    along = reach * tilt_cos - rise * tilt_sin
    up = reach * tilt_sin + rise * tilt_cos
    position.x = pivot_x - dir_x * along
    position.y = pivot_y - up
    position.z = pivot_z - dir_z * along

# The block lets go of whatever it was tipping or sliding on and falls, carrying the motion it had: a slide goes on the way it was going, a tip drifts the way the
# block was falling and goes on turning the way it was tipping. A block that is falling already keeps what it has.
func release():
    if mode == 3:
        vx = dir_x * slide_side * spin
        vz = dir_z * slide_side * spin
        spin = -slide_side * launch_spin * 0.5   # the end that is over nothing dips
    elif mode == 2:
        vx = -dir_x * tilt_sign * launch_push
        vz = -dir_z * tilt_sign * launch_push
        spin = tilt_sign * launch_spin
    set_mode(1)

# The block starts to go over. `side` is the way it goes (+1 toward the shape's +Z end, -1 toward its -Z end) and `edge` how far along its length (from its
# centre, signed) it would tip about. Unloaded, it tips: the far end drops, the near end rises. With something on top of it the rising side would push up into
# the load, so it slides out from under it instead. One that is already going keeps the speed it has built up.
func go_over(side: float, edge: float):
    if mode == 2 or mode == 3:
        return
    if age < calm:
        # Still settling: the blocks above may not have landed yet. It sleeps and looks again.
        set_mode(0)
        vx = 0.0
        vy = 0.0
        vz = 0.0
        spin = 0.0
        return
    var reach = half_length - end_inset
    var roof = probe_solid(half_height + look_below, -reach, 0.0, reach)   # what is on top of it, at the same three places
    if roof == 0:
        set_mode(2)
        spin = tip_start
        tilt_sign = -side
        pivot = edge
        roof_timer = 0.12
    else:
        set_mode(3)
        spin = slide_start
        slide_side = side

# Looks at what holds the block and picks what it does next.
func look():
    var reach = half_length - end_inset
    var held = probe_solid(-(half_height + look_below), -reach, 0.0, reach)   # 1 = the -Z end, 2 = middle, 4 = the +Z end, added up
    timer = check_interval
    if held == 0:
        release()   # nothing holds it: it falls (from a tip or a slide, carrying the motion it had)
    elif held == 1:
        go_over(1.0, -reach)          # held at the -Z end: that end stays and the +Z end drops
    elif held == 4:
        go_over(-1.0, reach)
    elif held == 2:
        if mode == 0 or mode == 1:
            # Balanced on one block: it goes over the edge of that block on the side that lost its support most recently. Whichever end it had held before
            # is the one that has gone; if it had neither (or both), a hash of its height decides, so blocks of one layer go the same way.
            var toward = sin(position.y * 137.0)
            if last_held == 4 or last_held == 6:
                toward = 1.0
            elif last_held == 1 or last_held == 3:
                toward = -1.0
            if toward >= 0.0:
                go_over(1.0, balance_reach)
            else:
                go_over(-1.0, -balance_reach)
    else:
        # Both ends are held, or the middle and an end: it stays. A block that has been tipping may have gone a little way into what it landed on (a tip is
        # placed, not collision-tested, and a body inside a solid shape can't go down or sideways): it is lifted a hair and dropped again onto its supports.
        spin = 0.0
        vx = 0.0
        vy = 0.0
        vz = 0.0
        slide_side = 0.0
        if mode == 2:
            position.y += settle_lift
            set_mode(1)
        else:
            set_mode(0)
    if mode == 3:
        timer = slide_look
    last_held = held

func _process(delta):
    age += delta
    if gone:
        return
    if Input.is_button_pressed("b"):
        reset_block()

    # Several blocks moving at once: the tower is coming down. Every block at rest is thrown, once.
    if $Tower.position.x < 0.5:
        joined = false
    elif age > calm and mode == 0 and not dragging and not joined:
        blow()
    if age > calm and $Tower.position.x < 0.5 and $Tower.position.y >= collapse_count:
        $Tower.position.x = 1.0

    # Pick the block up: the stylus went down on its touch area. Remember where on the flat plane through the block the stylus points,
    # relative to the block, so the block stays under the same spot of the stylus while it is dragged.
    if $JengaTouch.is_touch_pressed():
        dragging = true
        drag_y = position.y
        grab_dx = position.x - Input.touch_ground_x(drag_y)
        grab_dz = position.z - Input.touch_ground_z(drag_y)
        set_tilt(0.0)
        vx = 0.0
        vy = 0.0
        vz = 0.0
        spin = 0.0

    # Let go when the stylus is lifted: it falls until something holds it.
    if dragging and not Input.is_touching():
        dragging = false
        set_mode(1)

    if dragging:
        # Follow the stylus along the plane: move toward where it points (in steps of at most max_drag_step), stopped by solid shapes.
        var step_x = clamp(Input.touch_ground_x(drag_y) + grab_dx - position.x, -max_drag_step, max_drag_step)
        var step_z = clamp(Input.touch_ground_z(drag_y) + grab_dz - position.z, -max_drag_step, max_drag_step)
        move_and_collide(step_x, 0.0, step_z)
        return

    if mode == 0:
        # At rest: nothing moves and nothing is tested; every so often look again in case what held it has gone.
        timer -= delta
        if timer <= 0.0:
            look()
        return

    if mode == 3:
        # Sliding out from under its load along its length, faster and faster; every so often it looks to see whether it is held again or has come off.
        spin = min(spin + slide_accel * delta, max_slide)
        var travel = slide_side * spin * delta
        move_and_collide(dir_x * travel, 0.0, dir_z * travel)
        timer -= delta
        if timer <= 0.0:
            look()
        return

    if mode == 2:
        # Tipping about a held end or an edge: it turns faster and faster, and the far end drops.
        roof_timer -= delta
        if roof_timer <= 0.0:
            roof_timer = 0.12
            var reach = half_length - end_inset
            if probe_solid(half_height + look_below, -reach, 0.0, reach) != 0:
                # Something has come to rest on top of it: a tip would push its rising side up into that. It goes back down and slides out from under it instead.
                tilt_about(pivot, shape_lift - half_height, 0.0)
                set_mode(3)
                spin = slide_start
                slide_side = -tilt_sign
                return
        spin = min(spin + tip_accel * delta, max_spin)
        tilt_about(pivot, shape_lift - half_height, tilt + tilt_sign * spin * delta)
        if abs(tilt) > tip_limit and tilt * tilt_sign > 0.0:
            release()   # too far to be caught
        else:
            look()
        return

    # Falling, skidding or tumbling: gravity pulls it down, it goes on the way it was going, and it turns.
    if position.y < start_y - gone_depth:
        # Off the table and still falling: it is out of the game (it would count as a moving block for ever).
        gone = true
        set_mode(0)
        return
    vy = max(vy + gravity * delta, -max_fall)
    move_and_collide(vx * delta, vy * delta, vz * delta)
    var landed = is_on_floor()
    if is_on_wall():
        vx = -vx * bounce
        vz = -vz * bounce
    if spin != 0.0:
        # A turn about its centre, refused when it would push a corner into something: a move down of a hair is refused exactly when the block touches or is
        # inside something. Turned back, it bounces.
        var before = tilt
        tilt_about(0.0, shape_lift, tilt + spin * delta)
        move_and_collide(0.0, -0.003, 0.0)
        if is_on_floor():
            tilt_about(0.0, shape_lift, before)
            spin = -spin * bounce
        else:
            position.y += 0.003
    if landed:
        # On something: it stops falling and skids to a stop. A hard landing knocks it sideways and sets it turning a little, so blocks that land on one another
        # end up out of true, some of them held badly, and a fall through a tower stirs it up instead of restacking it neatly.
        if vy < -impact_speed:
            var shake = sin(age * 97.0 + position.x * 41.0 + position.z * 29.0)
            var shake2 = cos(age * 71.0 + position.z * 37.0 + position.y * 23.0)
            vx += shake * impact_kick
            vz += shake2 * impact_kick
            spin += shake * shake2 * launch_spin * 0.3
        vy = 0.0
        var keep = max(1.0 - ground_friction * delta, 0.0)
        vx *= keep
        vz *= keep
        spin *= keep
        if abs(vx) + abs(vz) < rest_speed and abs(spin) < 25.0:
            vx = 0.0
            vz = 0.0
            spin = 0.0
            look()
            if mode == 1:
                # It has come to rest on something the three probes don't see: an edge under its very tip, or a corner of a tilted block. It goes over that
                # edge: the way it was tilted, or the way it was sliding, or the way a hash of its height says.
                var fall_side = slide_side
                if tilt > 0.0:
                    fall_side = 1.0
                elif tilt < 0.0:
                    fall_side = -1.0
                elif fall_side == 0.0:
                    fall_side = 1.0
                    if sin(position.y * 137.0) < 0.0:
                        fall_side = -1.0
                go_over(fall_side, -fall_side * (half_length - 0.05))
